From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: TheDBP <TheDBP@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 24 Sep 2026 09:41:53 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] ether: VoLTE resources under the SIM's MNC, not the network's

Mint runs on T-Mobile but issues its own SIMs. Android selects resource mcc/mnc qualifiers from
the SIM, not from the serving network, and on this handset gsm.sim.operator.numeric reads 310240
while gsm.operator.numeric reads 310260. The values-mcc310-mnc260 overlay therefore never applied
and config_device_volte_available fell back to the AOSP default of false.

That one resource killed the entire IMS stack. ImsManager.isVolteEnabledByPlatform() ANDs it with
carrier_volte_available_bool, so CAPABILITY_TYPE_VOICE never entered the CapabilityChangeRequest.
MmTelFeatureCompatAdapter consequently asked the 7.1 vendor stack to enable
FEATURE_TYPE_UT_OVER_LTE and nothing else, the modem never attempted registration, and
UNSOL_RESPONSE_IMS_NETWORK_STATE_CHANGED carried Registration.state = 2 (NOT_REGISTERED) with
errorCode 0 for the whole session. ImsRegistrationCompatAdapter had nothing to report but
registrationDisconnected, so ImsPhone was never selected and dialling fell through to
GsmCdmaCallTracker and failed with INVALID_MODEM_STATE.

Carrier config was not at fault: mConfigFromDefaultApp supplies carrier_volte_available_bool=true
for this SIM. Only the device leg was false.

Proven on hardware before writing the file, with persist.dbg.volte_avail_ovr=1 standing in for the
resource, since that override is read ahead of both legs: the framework then enabled cap 0 on
radioTech 13, the modem walked Registration.state 2 -> 3 -> 1 (REGISTERED, radioTech 14), SST
logged setImsRegistrationState registered=true, ImsPhoneCallTracker logged isVolteEnabled=true and
imsdatadaemon started. The property is not shipped; it forces VoLTE on for every carrier, whereas
the overlay is scoped to the SIM that needs it.

The CarrierConfig overlay in overlay/packages/apps/CarrierConfig carries the same mnc=260
assumption. It is left alone deliberately: its keys are already satisfied by the carrier's own
bundle here, and changing them would add an unattributable variable to a state now known good.
---
 .../res/res/values-mcc310-mnc240/config.xml   | 24 +++++++++++++++++++
 .../res/res/values-mcc310-mnc260/config.xml   |  4 +++-
 2 files changed, 27 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)
 create mode 100644 overlay/frameworks/base/core/res/res/values-mcc310-mnc240/config.xml

diff --git a/overlay/frameworks/base/core/res/res/values-mcc310-mnc240/config.xml b/overlay/frameworks/base/core/res/res/values-mcc310-mnc240/config.xml
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..9658dd3
--- /dev/null
+++ b/overlay/frameworks/base/core/res/res/values-mcc310-mnc240/config.xml
@@ -0,0 +1,24 @@
+<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
+<!-- Mint Mobile (MCC 310 / MNC 240) VoLTE / VT / Wi-Fi-Calling.
+
+     Mint runs on T-Mobile's network but issues its own SIMs, and Android picks resource
+     mcc/mnc qualifiers from the SIM, not from the serving network. On this device
+     gsm.sim.operator.numeric reads 310240 while gsm.operator.numeric reads 310260, so the
+     values-mcc310-mnc260 overlay never applied and config_device_volte_available fell back to
+     the AOSP default of false. ImsManager.isVolteEnabledByPlatform() ANDs that resource with
+     carrier_volte_available_bool, so VOICE never entered the CapabilityChangeRequest, the
+     vendor stack was only ever told to enable UT over LTE, and the modem never registered.
+
+     Carrier config was not the problem: mConfigFromDefaultApp supplies
+     carrier_volte_available_bool=true for this SIM. Only the device leg was false.
+
+     Keep this file in step with values-mcc310-mnc260/config.xml. -->
+<resources>
+    <bool name="config_device_volte_available">true</bool>
+    <bool name="config_device_vt_available">true</bool>
+    <bool name="config_device_wfc_ims_available">true</bool>
+    <!-- legacy leg some code paths still consult -->
+    <bool name="config_carrier_volte_available">true</bool>
+    <!-- displayed RAT during a VoLTE call; 3 = UMTS, T-Mobile's value -->
+    <integer name="config_volte_replacement_rat">3</integer>
+</resources>
diff --git a/overlay/frameworks/base/core/res/res/values-mcc310-mnc260/config.xml b/overlay/frameworks/base/core/res/res/values-mcc310-mnc260/config.xml
index cbb6857..7cbbfb1 100644
--- a/overlay/frameworks/base/core/res/res/values-mcc310-mnc260/config.xml
+++ b/overlay/frameworks/base/core/res/res/values-mcc310-mnc260/config.xml
@@ -1,5 +1,7 @@
 <?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
-<!-- T-Mobile US (MCC 310 / MNC 260, includes Mint Mobile) VoLTE / VT / Wi-Fi-Calling.
+<!-- T-Mobile US (MCC 310 / MNC 260) VoLTE / VT / Wi-Fi-Calling. NOT Mint: Mint SIMs
+     report 310240 and are handled by the values-mcc310-mnc240 sibling, because Android
+     takes these qualifiers from the SIM and not from the serving network.
      Android applies this values-mcc310-mnc260 overlay ONLY when a 310/260 SIM is present,
      so it is inherently scoped to T-Mobile's network — no other carrier is touched.
 
